edb/ref 17592186251142 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251144 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1089 "q-302142" 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1100 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-3x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-3\\le x<1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-bx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=4.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1113 #uuid "94f1b338-29da-5fa4-8842-fa97970f2c94" 13194139533788 true] [17592186251141 1145 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $4,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $4.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $-3\\le x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}-3x+2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =a({1}^{2})-3(1)+2 \\\\  & =a-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ a-1 & =4 \\\\ a & =4+1 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $1\\le x\\le 3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-bx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}-bx) \\\\  & =3({1}^{2})-b(1) \\\\  & =3-b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ 3-b & =4 \\\\ b & =3-4 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533788 true] [17592186251142 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251143 13194139533788 true] [17592186251142 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533788 true] [17592186251142 1041 "field-1" 13194139533788 true] [17592186251142 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251143 13194139533788 true] [17592186251142 1110 #uuid "9b55d56d-946c-5